  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Show that each function is increasing on each interval of its domain.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y=3x-5$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=3>0$ for all real $x$.",
          "worked": "$$y'=3>0$$\nSo the function is increasing for all real $x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 11
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y=\\sqrt{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}>0$ for $x>0$. The function increases on $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*} y'&=\\tfrac12 x^{-\\frac12}\\\\y'&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}>0\\end{align*}$$\nSo the function increases for $x>0$. Also, $y(0)=0$ and $\\sqrt{x}>0$ for $x>0$, so it increases on $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q2-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=\\frac{3x}{4-x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=\\frac{12}{(4-x)^2}>0$ for $x\\ne 4$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{3(4-x)+3x}{(4-x)^2}\\\\y'&=\\frac{12}{(4-x)^2}>0\\end{align*}$$\nThe denominator is positive for $x\\ne 4$. The function increases on $(-\\infty,4)$ and $(4,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q2-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$y=\\frac{x^2-1}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2}>0$ for $x\\ne 0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{2x^2-(x^2-1)}{x^2}\\\\y'&=\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2}>0\\end{align*}$$\nBoth numerator and denominator are positive for $x\\ne 0$. The function increases on $(-\\infty,0)$ and $(0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      }
    ]
  },
